        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- Louer = both to rent (as a tenant) and to rent out (as an owner): Je loue un appartement. Je loue ma maison à des étudiants. Le loyer = the rent. Meublé = furnished, non meublé = unfurnished.\n- Floors: le rez-de-chaussée (ground floor), le premier étage (1st floor above the ground), le deuxième étage… This is the British system: the American “first floor” = le rez-de-chaussée.\n- French sizes in advertisements use T (type): T2 = two main rooms (living room + one bedroom), T3 = three main rooms. The kitchen and bathroom are not counted. Sometimes F2, F3, or “deux-pièces”, “trois-pièces”.\n- La salle de bain has a bath (une baignoire); la salle d'eau has a shower only. Les toilettes / les WC are plural: Les toilettes sont à droite. (In French the toilet is often separate from the bathroom.)\n- Un salon is the living room (also called le séjour, mainly in ads). Un bureau means desk or study. Une cuisine américaine = open-plan kitchen, une cuisine équipée = fitted with appliances.\n- Ordinals: premier / première (1st), then add -ième to the number: deuxième, troisième, quatrième; cinq → cinquième (add u), neuf → neuvième (f → v). Abbreviations: 1er, 2e, 3e (not “2nd”).\n- Beau / belle / bel: bel before a masculine vowel: un bel appartement, un bel immeuble. Ancien before a noun can mean “former”: mon ancien appartement, but un immeuble ancien = an old building (after the noun)."
        },
